        resource_name='' (required): Name of the resource or resource set to release.
        worker_when_blocked='leaves': What the worker does while this station holds a finished part that nothing downstream has room for. "leaves" (the default): the worker is free when the work ends. "stays": the worker waits with the part and is free when it is accepted downstream. The same either way where nothing downstream is finite.
        """

    def batch(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        batch_size: int | dict[str, Any] = 2,
        batched_entity_type: str | dict[str, Any] = 'BATCH',
        max_wait: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        max_wait_time_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        carrier_type: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        carrier_capacity: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        member_time: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        member_time_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a BATCH block. Groups parts or orders into batches, pallets or lots of a set size, or after a maximum wait, and sends the batch on as one entity. Groups entities by count: puts each arrival in the block's store and, as soon as `batch_size` items are waiting, takes exactly that many atomically to form one batch entity. Members leave live_entities and carry `in_batch` until a SEPARATE splits the batch again. With `max_wait` set, a PARTIAL batch leaves too: the timer is armed by the arrival that starts a new group and releases whatever is waiting once the oldest member has waited that long. TIE RULE: a batch that fills in the same instant as the timer expires wins and the timer releases nothing -- the same zero-delay re-check reneging uses, so the outcome never depends on the order SimPy happened to schedule two events at one timestamp. With `carrier_type` set, an arrival of that type is a CARRIER (a truck): it is not stored, and loads the stored arrivals one at a time, `member_time` each, until it holds `carrier_capacity` (default `batch_size`) or its `max_wait`, counted from its own arrival, has passed; then it leaves as itself, its members aboard.

        batch_size=2: Number of entities grouped into one batch entity.
        batched_entity_type='BATCH': Entity type assigned to the batch entity.
        max_wait='': How long a partial batch waits before it leaves anyway, e.g. "30" or "entity.deadline"; measured from the arrival that started the group. A batch that fills in the same instant wins. Empty, 0 or less = wait for a full batch for ever.
        max_wait_time_unit='base': Unit `max_wait` is expressed in: "base" (the model's own unit) or one of seconds/minutes/hours/days.
        carrier_type='': Carrier mode: an arriving entity of this type (a truck) is a CARRIER that waits here and loads the other arrivals one at a time, until it is full or its max_wait (the cut-off, measured from the carrier's arrival) runs out, then leaves as itself. Empty = a plain BATCH.
        carrier_capacity='': Carrier mode: how many members a carrier takes, e.g. "entity.capacity", evaluated for each carrier when it arrives. Empty = batch_size.
        member_time='': Carrier mode: time to load one member, evaluated in the member's own scope when its load starts. Empty = loading takes no time.
        member_time_unit='base': Unit `member_time` is expressed in: "base" (the model's own unit) or one of seconds/minutes/hours/days.
        """

    def match(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        number_to_match: int | dict[str, Any] = 2,
        batch_option: bool | dict[str, Any] = False,
        batched_entity_type: str | dict[str, Any] = 'BATCH',
        type_store_map: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        match_on: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a MATCH block. Waits until one entity of each of several kinds is present (a part and its kit, an order and its customer), then releases them together. Match `number_to_match` *distinct* entity types; with `match_on` set, only entities whose value of that attribute is equal (Arena's attribute match). The arriving entity is always stored first, so it can never be dropped ; the match is then taken atomically from the stores, oldest waiting type first, FIFO within a type (and within a value).

        number_to_match=2: Number of distinct entity types to match together.
        batch_option=False: True: combine the matched entities into one batch entity. False: forward them separately.
        batched_entity_type='BATCH': Entity type assigned to the batch entity, when batch_option is True.
        type_store_map={}: Optional {e_type: store index} mapping; default is automatic assignment.
        match_on='': An entity attribute (e.g. "order"); entities are matched only with others whose value of it is equal -- Arena's attribute match -- still one per distinct type, FIFO within a value. Empty = match by type alone.
        """

    def separate(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        mode: str | dict[str, Any] = 'Retain original entity values',
        attrs: list[Any] | dict[str, Any] = ...,
        carrier: str | dict[str, Any] = 'dispose',
        member_time: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        member_time_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        release_order: str | dict[str, Any] = 'fifo',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a SEPARATE block. Splits a batch, pallet or lot back into its individual parts, orders or customers, optionally copying the batch's attributes to each. Parameters ---------- mode : {'retain','all','specific'} - 'retain' children keep their own attributes - 'all' copy *all* parent attributes to each child - 'specific' copy only the names listed in *attrs* attrs : list[str] | str | None Comma-separated string or list used when *mode* == 'specific'. carrier : {'dispose','keep'} 'keep': the parent is a CARRIER (a truck) that lives on. Its members leave one at a time on exit 0, each after its own `member_time`, and the carrier leaves on exit 1 once it is empty.

        mode='Retain original entity values': How children get their attributes: retain their own values, take all of the parent's, or take only the ones listed in attrs.
        attrs=[]: Attribute labels to copy from the parent, used when mode is "specific".
        carrier='dispose': "dispose": the batch ends here and its parts leave together. "keep": the batch is a carrier (a truck) that lives on -- its members leave one at a time by the first link, each after member_time, and the carrier leaves by the SECOND link once it is empty.
        member_time='': Keep mode only: time for one member to come off, e.g. "2" or "TRIA(1, 2, 3)", evaluated for each member in its own scope when its turn starts. Empty = every member leaves in the same instant.
        member_time_unit='base': Unit `member_time` is expressed in: "base" (the model's own unit) or one of seconds/minutes/hours/days.
        release_order='fifo': Order the members leave in: "fifo" (the order they were loaded) or "lifo" (last loaded first off, a rear-door truck).
        """

    def duplicate(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        number_of_duplicates: int | dict[str, Any] = 2,
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a DUPLICATE block. Makes copies of an entity, for example to model one job spawning several; the original goes on unchanged. All copies leave by the one output link: follow it with a DECIDE to send them down different branches. Forwards the original entity unchanged, then forwards `number_of_duplicates - 1` shallow copies, each a fresh Entity with its own id and name (never a deepcopy: that used to clone the entity manager and any live resource requests).

        number_of_duplicates=2: Total number of entities leaving this block, original included (>= 2).
        """

    def hold(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        mode: str | dict[str, Any] = 'Signal',
        signal_name: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        conditions: list[Any] | dict[str, Any] = ...,
        scan_interval: float | dict[str, Any] = 1.0,
        scan_interval_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a HOLD block. Holds entities waiting in a queue until a named signal fires, a condition becomes true (a buffer has room, a machine is idle), or for good. Infinite mode never releases: the entity stays held in the store for the rest of the run. That is the documented semantics, not a leak.

        mode='Signal': "Signal": wait for a named signal. "Condition": wait until every condition is true, re-checked whenever something it reads changes. "Infinite": never release.
        signal_name='': Name of the signal to wait for, when mode is "Signal".
        conditions=[]: Boolean expressions that must all be true to release, when mode is "Condition".
        scan_interval=1.0: Fallback re-check interval, when mode is "Condition": used only when a condition reads something no event announces (TNOW, TS(), RAND(), a level between events). Variables, NQ/NR/MR/IDLE, NS and threshold writes wake it at once.
        scan_interval_unit='base': Unit of `scan_interval`: "base" (the model's) or one of its quantity's units.
        """

    def signal(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        distribution: str | Distribution = 'Constant',
        dist_params: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        time_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        signal_name: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a SIGNAL block. Broadcasts a named signal that releases every entity waiting at a HOLD block for it, for example a shift start, a gate opening or a green light. After an optional delay, succeeds the named signal event (releasing every HOLD block currently waiting on it) and immediately replaces it with a fresh one -- broadcast, edge-triggered, auto-reset.

        distribution='Constant': Name of a registered distribution (see the distribution reference).
        dist_params={'value': 0.0}: Parameters for `distribution`, by name (e.g. {"rate": 0.5} for Exponential).
        time_unit='base': Unit the sampled duration is expressed in: "base" (the model's own unit, no conversion) or one of seconds/minutes/hours/days.
        signal_name='': Name of the signal to succeed; matches a HOLD block's signal_name.
        """

    def logic(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        logic_class: str | dict[str, Any] = 'SampleLogic',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a LOGIC block. Custom routing written in Python: sends each entity to one of several outgoing links by your own rule when chance or a condition is not enough. Routes an entity through a user-supplied, plain (non-BaseProcess) class defined in nagasim.core.custom_logic, or first in the module the caller passes as `run_model(logic=...)`. The class only needs an `execute(self, entity, ctx) -> int` method returning the outgoing link index; `execute(self, entity)` (no ctx) is also accepted.

        logic_class='SampleLogic': Name of a class in nagasim.core.custom_logic implementing execute(entity, ctx) -> int.
        """

    def process(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        action: str | dict[str, Any] = 'Seize Delay Release',
        distribution: str | Distribution = 'Constant',
        dist_params: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        time_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        priority: int | dict[str, Any] = 0,
        quantity: int | dict[str, Any] = 1,
        resource_name: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        setup: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        setup_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        setup_key: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        initial_key: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        setup_cost: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        setup_cost_category: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        balk_if: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        renege_after: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        renege_time_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        preempt: bool | dict[str, Any] = False,
        queue_capacity: int | dict[str, Any] = 0,
        hold_until_space: bool | dict[str, Any] = False,
        space_of: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        worker_fleet: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        worker_skill: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        node: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        worker_when_blocked: str | dict[str, Any] = 'leaves',
        on_preempt: str | dict[str, Any] = 'resume',
        resample: bool | dict[str, Any] = False,
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a PROCESS block. Machine, workstation or service step: a part, order or customer waits for a resource (machine, operator, server), is processed for a time, then frees it. Seize, delay and release fused in one block; `action` selects which of the three steps run ("Seize Delay Release", "Seize Delay", "Delay Release", "Delay"), so the same block type covers a self-contained service, the start or end of a hold split across two blocks, or a plain timed delay. The most-used block for a simple resource-backed service step.

        action='Seize Delay Release': Which of seize/delay/release run: "Seize Delay Release", "Seize Delay", "Delay Release" or "Delay".
        distribution='Constant': Name of a registered distribution (see the distribution reference).
        dist_params={'value': 0.0}: Parameters for `distribution`, by name (e.g. {"rate": 0.5} for Exponential).
        time_unit='base': Unit the sampled duration is expressed in: "base" (the model's own unit, no conversion) or one of seconds/minutes/hours/days.
        priority=0: Lower draws first when the resource's queue is contested. One number for the block (an entity attribute is not read): to rank entity types, route each to its own block on the shared resource.
        quantity=1: Number of resource units requested/released.
        resource_name='': Name of the resource or resource set; required unless action is "Delay" or a worker_fleet does the work alone.
        setup='': Sequence-dependent setup time: an expression charged after the seize when this block's resource changes over, e.g. TABLE("Setups", previous_key, key). `previous_key` and `key` are in scope here only. Empty = no setup.
        setup_unit='base': Unit of `setup`: "base" (the model's) or one of its quantity's units.
        setup_key='': Expression identifying what a job is set up for; empty uses the entity type. A setup is charged only when it differs from the previous job's key at this block.
        initial_key='': What the resource is already set up for at time 0; empty means the first job is charged no setup.
        setup_cost='': Money charged ONCE PER CHANGEOVER, not per job -- an expression like TABLE("Changeover cost", previous_key, key), with `previous_key` and `key` in scope exactly as in "setup". It becomes an event cost item named after the block, joins Total Cost and resets at warm-up. Empty = free changeovers.
        setup_cost_category='': Cost category the changeover cost is grouped under in the cost_summary frame (default "Changeover").
        balk_if='': Condition evaluated on arrival; when true the entity does not join the queue and leaves by the second outgoing link, e.g. "queue_length >= 5". `queue_length` is the queue it would have joined. Empty = never balk.
        renege_after='': How long the entity waits before giving up, e.g. "EXPO(15)" or "entity.patience"; it then leaves by the second outgoing link. A grant in the same instant wins. Empty = wait for ever.
        renege_time_unit='base': Unit `renege_after` is expressed in: "base" (the model's own unit) or one of seconds/minutes/hours/days.
        preempt=False: When no unit is free, interrupt the lowest-priority job in service on this resource (a strictly higher priority wins). Only with action "Seize Delay Release", and not on a resource set.
        queue_capacity=0: Places in this station's own input queue. 0 (the default) is an unlimited queue; with a positive value a part takes a place on arrival and frees it when service starts, so an upstream machine with nowhere to put its finished part stays blocked. `balk_if` sees the places taken as `queue_length`, which is how a LOSS system is written instead of a blocking one.
        hold_until_space=False: Blocking BEFORE service: after seizing, do not start the job until the downstream space has a free place, and reserve it for this part. The station waits idle (reported as Blocked %). Needs a downstream BUFFER with capacity > 0 or a SEIZE/PROCESS with queue_capacity > 0. Off (the default) is blocking AFTER service.
        space_of='': Which block's space `hold_until_space` reserves in; empty = the target of this block's first outgoing link. The part must reach that block.
        worker_fleet='': A fleet with kind "worker". After this block's own resource (if any) is seized, a worker is requested, walks EMPTY on the network to `node`, and is held for the service. Empty = no worker.
        worker_skill='': Only a worker whose skills contain this name may serve; empty = any worker of the fleet.
        node='': Network node this block sits on; required with worker_fleet (where the worker walks to).
        worker_when_blocked='leaves': What the worker does while this station holds a finished part that nothing downstream has room for. "leaves" (the default): the worker is free when the work ends. "stays": the worker waits with the part and is free when it is accepted downstream. The same either way where nothing downstream is finite.
        on_preempt='resume': What THIS block's job does when something interrupts it and it gets the resource back: "resume" (finish the remaining time) or "restart" (the whole service again, same sample). Set on the block being interrupted, not on the one doing the interrupting.
        resample=False: With on_preempt "restart" only: draw a FRESH service time from this block's own random stream on each restart, instead of repeating the same sample. Off (the default) repeats it.
        """

    def motion(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
