        priority=0: Lower draws first when the resource's queue is contested. One number for the block (an entity attribute is not read): to rank entity types, route each to its own block on the shared resource.
        quantity=1: Number of resource units requested/released.
        resource_name='': Name of the resource or resource set; required unless action is "Delay" or a worker_fleet does the work alone.
        setup='': Sequence-dependent setup time: an expression charged after the seize when this block's resource changes over, e.g. TABLE("Setups", previous_key, key). `previous_key` and `key` are in scope here only. Empty = no setup.
        setup_unit='base': Unit of `setup`: "base" (the model's) or one of its quantity's units.
        setup_key='': Expression identifying what a job is set up for; empty uses the entity type. A setup is charged only when it differs from the previous job's key at this block.
        initial_key='': What the resource is already set up for at time 0; empty means the first job is charged no setup.
        setup_cost='': Money charged ONCE PER CHANGEOVER, not per job -- an expression like TABLE("Changeover cost", previous_key, key), with `previous_key` and `key` in scope exactly as in "setup". It becomes an event cost item named after the block, joins Total Cost and resets at warm-up. Empty = free changeovers.
        setup_cost_category='': Cost category the changeover cost is grouped under in the cost_summary frame (default "Changeover").
        balk_if='': Condition evaluated on arrival; when true the entity does not join the queue and leaves by the second outgoing link, e.g. "queue_length >= 5". `queue_length` is the queue it would have joined. Empty = never balk.
        renege_after='': How long the entity waits before giving up, e.g. "EXPO(15)" or "entity.patience"; it then leaves by the second outgoing link. A grant in the same instant wins. Empty = wait for ever.
        renege_time_unit='base': Unit `renege_after` is expressed in: "base" (the model's own unit) or one of seconds/minutes/hours/days.
        preempt=False: When no unit is free, interrupt the lowest-priority job in service on this resource (a strictly higher priority wins). Only with action "Seize Delay Release", and not on a resource set.
        queue_capacity=0: Places in this station's own input queue. 0 (the default) is an unlimited queue; with a positive value a part takes a place on arrival and frees it when service starts, so an upstream machine with nowhere to put its finished part stays blocked. `balk_if` sees the places taken as `queue_length`, which is how a LOSS system is written instead of a blocking one.
        hold_until_space=False: Blocking BEFORE service: after seizing, do not start the job until the downstream space has a free place, and reserve it for this part. The station waits idle (reported as Blocked %). Needs a downstream BUFFER with capacity > 0 or a SEIZE/PROCESS with queue_capacity > 0. Off (the default) is blocking AFTER service.
        space_of='': Which block's space `hold_until_space` reserves in; empty = the target of this block's first outgoing link. The part must reach that block.
        worker_fleet='': A fleet with kind "worker". After this block's own resource (if any) is seized, a worker is requested, walks EMPTY on the network to `node`, and is held for the service. Empty = no worker.
        worker_skill='': Only a worker whose skills contain this name may serve; empty = any worker of the fleet.
        node='': Network node this block sits on; required with worker_fleet (where the worker walks to).
        worker_when_blocked='leaves': What the worker does while this station holds a finished part that nothing downstream has room for. "leaves" (the default): the worker is free when the work ends. "stays": the worker waits with the part and is free when it is accepted downstream. The same either way where nothing downstream is finite.
        on_preempt='resume': What THIS block's job does when something interrupts it and it gets the resource back: "resume" (finish the remaining time) or "restart" (the whole service again, same sample). Set on the block being interrupted, not on the one doing the interrupting.
        resample=False: With on_preempt "restart" only: draw a FRESH service time from this block's own random stream on each restart, instead of repeating the same sample. Off (the default) repeats it.
        """

    def record(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        stat_name: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        kind: str | dict[str, Any] = 'count',
        expr: str | dict[str, Any] = '1',
        category: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a RECORD block. Counts or measures something as entities pass (a count, a time between arrivals, a defect, a cost) in a user-named statistic, without delaying them. A zero-time block that updates a user-defined statistic and forwards the entity unchanged. Statistics live on the simulation context (`ctx.user_stats`), keyed by `stat_name`, so two RECORD blocks writing to the same name share one collector -- core/schema/ rejects a same-name pair with different `kind`s at load time, before a run can hit the mismatch here. `expr` is the increment for "count", the observed value for "tally", the new level for "time_persistent", and ignored for "time_between" (it tallies the gap since the statistic's last arrival; the first arrival records nothing). `kind="cost"` is the one kind that builds no collector: `expr` is a MONEY amount added to the cost item `stat_name` every time an entity passes -- an ordering cost, a changeover cost, a lost-sale penalty. It joins the model's Total Cost and is itemised in the `cost_summary` frame.

        stat_name='' (required): Name of the user statistic; two RECORD blocks with the same name share one collector.
        kind='count': "count" (running total of expr), "tally" (mean of expr), "time_between" (mean gap between arrivals), "time_persistent" (time-average level of expr) or "cost" (adds expr to a named cost item and to Total Cost).
        expr='1': Expression evaluated at each arrival; ignored for kind=time_between.
        category='': Cost category this item is grouped under in the cost_summary frame; used by kind=cost only.
        """

    def station(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        station: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        node: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        tending: dict[str, Any] = ...,
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a STATION block. Named stop on a job-shop or multi-step routing where a ROUTE block delivers parts, orders or jobs and sets their current location. Named entry point of a routing. Entities arrive here from a ROUTE block whose sequence step names this block, or along an ordinary link; either way `entity.station` becomes this block's name and the entity continues along the station's single outgoing link.

        station='': Optional station name this block answers to, shown as `entity.station` and usable in a sequence step. Empty: the block's own name is the station name.
        node='': Optional path-network node this station sits on. With one, travel to and from this station is the shortest path along the network and `DISTANCE("...", "...")` can name it.
        tending={}: Machine tending by a fleet with an `arm`: {"place": [segments], "pick": [segments], "time_unit": "seconds", "hrc": "none", "zone": {}, "cobot": "leave"|"stay"}. Segments, hrc and zone are a MOTION block's, timed with the fleet's arm profile; a missing cycle uses the fleet's flat unload/load time. The machine keeps a finished part until the cobot has picked it, and the cobot holds a part until the machine accepts it; "stay" keeps the cobot at the station until the part moves on. Empty: an ordinary station.
        """

    def route(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        distribution: str | Distribution = 'Constant',
        dist_params: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        time_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        travel: str | dict[str, Any] = 'time',
        speed: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        speed_unit: Literal['base', 'mm/ms', 'mm/s', 'mm/min', 'mm/h', 'mm/day', 'mm/week', 'mm/month', 'mm/year', 'cm/ms', 'cm/s', 'cm/min', 'cm/h', 'cm/day', 'cm/week', 'cm/month', 'cm/year', 'm/ms', 'm/s', 'm/min', 'm/h', 'm/day', 'm/week', 'm/month', 'm/year', 'km/ms', 'km/s', 'km/min', 'km/h', 'km/day', 'km/week', 'km/month', 'km/year'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a ROUTE block. Sends a part or job to the next station on its own routing sequence (a job-shop or multi-step route) instead of along a fixed link. Send the entity to the next station of its `sequence`, applying that step's attribute values and advancing its `step` index; once the sequence is finished, forward along the block's own outgoing link instead.

        distribution='Constant': Name of a registered distribution (see the distribution reference).
        dist_params={'value': 0.0}: Parameters for `distribution`, by name (e.g. {"rate": 0.5} for Exponential).
        time_unit='base': Unit the sampled duration is expressed in: "base" (the model's own unit, no conversion) or one of seconds/minutes/hours/days.
        travel='time': How the transfer time is found: "time" uses the distribution above, "network" divides the shortest path between the two stations' nodes by `speed`.
        speed='': Travel speed for `travel = "network"`, an expression in distance per time unit (e.g. 1.2, or `entity.walk_speed`). Empty means 1.
        speed_unit='base': Unit of `speed`: "base" (the model's) or one of its quantity's units.
        """

    def transfer(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        level: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        amount: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '1',
        mode: str | dict[str, Any] = 'remove',
        wait: bool | dict[str, Any] = False,
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a TRANSFER block. Adds to or takes from a continuous tank, stock or inventory level (fuel, material, a pool) as the entity passes, optionally waiting until it is available.

        level='' (required): Name of the level this block adds to or removes from.
        amount='1': How much to move: an expression, e.g. 20 or entity.quantity.
        mode='remove': "remove" takes the amount out of the level, "add" puts it in.
        wait=False: Wait until the level can pay the full amount (the backorder pattern); otherwise move what it can and record the rest as unmet.
        """

    def charge(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        energy: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '0',
        max_power: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '0',
        time_limit: float | str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        time_limit_unit: Literal['base', 'milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        time_limit_from: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        leave_when_full: bool | dict[str, Any] = True,
        site_limit: float | dict[str, Any] = 0.0,
        efficiency: float | dict[str, Any] = 1.0,
        sharing: str | dict[str, Any] = 'equal',
        power_variable: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a CHARGE block. Charges an entity (an electric vehicle, a battery pack) with the energy it needs at up to its own power, sharing a site power cap with every other entity at this block; it leaves when full or when its time is up. The block's total grid kW (what the entities take over `efficiency`) is kept in `power_variable`, so a flow with `rate: VAR("name")` puts it on the electricity bus; `site_limit` caps that grid kW. On leaving the entity carries `charged_kwh`, `charge_full` (1 or 0) and `charge_time` (entry to full, or to leaving when not full, in `time_limit_unit`).

        energy='0' (required): Energy the entity needs, kWh: an expression, e.g. entity.need_kwh.
        max_power='0' (required): Most power the entity takes, kW: an expression, e.g. min(11, entity.max_kw).
        time_limit='': Longest time the entity stays (an expression); it leaves then, full or not. Empty = until full.
        time_limit_unit='base': Unit of `time_limit` and of the `charge_time` the entity carries: "base" (the model's) or one of its quantity's units.
        time_limit_from='': Instant, in model time, the time limit counts from: an expression such as entity.arrived_at. Empty = when the entity enters. A deadline already past sends it on at once, charged 0.
        leave_when_full=True: Leave as soon as the need is met. Off: stay (drawing nothing) until the time limit, as a car stays plugged in.
        site_limit=0.0: Most power all entities at this block draw together from the grid, kW; 0 = no cap.
        efficiency=1.0: Energy the entity stores per unit drawn from the grid (charger losses); 1 = none. The site limit and power_variable are grid side.
        sharing='equal': How the cap is shared: "equal" (an equal share each; what a slow entity cannot use goes to the others) or "first_come" (in order of arrival, each as much as it can take).
        power_variable='': Model variable kept equal to the block's total grid kW, for a flow rate VAR("name") on the electricity bus. Empty = none.
        """

    def transport(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        fleet: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        destination: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        priority: int | dict[str, Any] = 0,
        keep_vehicle: bool | dict[str, Any] = False,
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a TRANSPORT block. Moves a part, order or load between locations with a vehicle (truck, AGV, forklift, robot) from a fleet: fetch, carry, unload, free the vehicle.

        fleet='' (required): Name of the mover fleet whose vehicles do the carrying.
        destination='': STATION the entity is carried to. Empty: the next station of the entity's routing, exactly as ROUTE would choose it.
        priority=0: Request priority for a vehicle; lower is served first, and outranks the fleet's dispatching rule.
        keep_vehicle=False: Keep the vehicle after the drop: it waits there, busy, and the entity's next TRANSPORT on this fleet drives it. A RELEASE naming the fleet, or the entity leaving the model, frees it.
        """

    def conveyor(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        length: float | dict[str, Any] = 0.0,
        length_unit: Literal['base', 'millimeters', 'centimeters', 'meters', 'kilometers'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        speed: float | dict[str, Any] = 1.0,
        speed_unit: Literal['base', 'mm/ms', 'mm/s', 'mm/min', 'mm/h', 'mm/day', 'mm/week', 'mm/month', 'mm/year', 'cm/ms', 'cm/s', 'cm/min', 'cm/h', 'cm/day', 'cm/week', 'cm/month', 'cm/year', 'm/ms', 'm/s', 'm/min', 'm/h', 'm/day', 'm/week', 'm/month', 'm/year', 'km/ms', 'km/s', 'km/min', 'km/h', 'km/day', 'km/week', 'km/month', 'km/year'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        spacing: float | dict[str, Any] = 1.0,
        spacing_unit: Literal['base', 'millimeters', 'centimeters', 'meters', 'kilometers'] | dict[str, Any] = 'base',
        accumulating: bool | dict[str, Any] = True,
        exit_resource: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        from_node: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        to_node: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        stops: list[Any] | dict[str, Any] = ...,
        diverts: list[Any] | dict[str, Any] = ...,
        merges: list[Any] | dict[str, Any] = ...,
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a CONVEYOR block. Conveyor belt that carries parts from one end to the other at a set speed, holds a limited number, and blocks the machine before it when full.

        length=0.0: Belt length in layout units. 0 takes the network distance between `from_node` and `to_node`.
        length_unit='base': Unit of `length`: "base" (the model's) or one of its quantity's units.
        speed=1.0 (required): Belt speed, length units per model time unit.
        speed_unit='base': Unit of `speed`: "base" (the model's) or one of its quantity's units.
        spacing=1.0 (required): Belt length one entity occupies; capacity is floor(length / spacing) and the entry rhythm is spacing / speed.
        spacing_unit='base': Unit of `spacing`: "base" (the model's) or one of its quantity's units.
        accumulating=True: True: a blocked entity stops and the others pack behind it. False: the whole belt stops while the exit is blocked.
        exit_resource='': Resource an entity must take to leave the belt -- the end stop that makes accumulation happen. It carries the resource onward, so release it downstream (a PROCESS with action "Delay Release"). Empty: no end stop. Either way, a belt that feeds another belt keeps its end part until that belt has room (back-pressure).
        from_node='': Path-network node the belt starts at (drawing, and the length when `length` is 0).
        to_node='': Path-network node the belt ends at.
        stops=[]: Points where each part halts on the belt: {"at": <distance from the entry, in the unit of `length`, between 0 and the length>, "time": <number or expression>, "resource"?: <seized for the stop>}. Parts behind pack against a stop on an accumulating belt; a non-accumulating belt stops while a part is at one. Empty: none.
        diverts=[]: Points where a part may leave the belt sideways: {"at": <distance from the entry, in the unit of `length`>, "condition": <expression, read once as the part reaches it>, "time"?: <push time, number or expression>, "when_full"?: "wait" (default: the part waits there for room) or "pass" (it rides on)}. Divert k leaves by outgoing link k + 1; link 0 is the belt's end. Empty: none.
        merges=[]: Points where parts from another block join the belt: {"at": <distance from the entry, in the unit of `length`>, "from": <the block linking to this belt whose parts join there>, "priority"?: "belt" (default: they wait for a gap of two spacings) or "merge" (parts on the belt make room)}. Accumulating belts only. Empty: none.
        """

    def buffer(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        capacity: int | dict[str, Any] = 1,
        level: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a BUFFER block. Finite storage of waiting places between two machines or stations; when all places are taken the upstream machine stays blocked, holding its finished part.

        capacity=1 (required): Places in the buffer, 0 or more. A part takes one on the way in and holds it until the next station accepts it, so an upstream machine blocks while the buffer is full. 0 means no place at all (blocking after service with no buffer); for an unlimited queue, do not draw a buffer.
        level='': Optional level whose value mirrors the buffer's content, so an asset can draw it as a fill indicator. Empty = no level.
        """

    def send(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        message: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        to: Any = 'entity.agent',
        payload: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a SEND block. Sends a message, with an optional payload, to agents of an agent-based model so they can react, for example a customer calling a driver.

        message='' (required): Name of the message; an agent's "message" transition matches it.
        to='entity.agent': Recipients: an expression giving an agent (entity.agent, AGENT("Pop", i)) or {"population": "Pop"[, "where": "<condition on self>"][, "random": true]}.
        payload='': Optional expression evaluated for the entity; the receiving transition reads it as `payload`.
        """

    def submodel(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        definition: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
        arguments: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        preset: str | dict[str, Any] = '',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a SUBMODEL block. Editor-only blocks (SUBMODEL, SUB_ENTRY, SUB_EXIT) that group blocks into a reusable sub-model with named ports, expanded away before the run. Editor-only placeholder shared by three types, never run directly -- `core/submodels.flatten()` expands them away before the engine builds any process. SUB_ENTRY: an input port of a submodel definition (`port` names it; exactly one outgoing link inside the definition, none incoming). SUB_EXIT: an output port of a definition (`port` names it; incoming links only). SUBMODEL: an instance of a definition dropped on the canvas (`definition` names it, `arguments` binds its parameters).

        definition='': Name of the submodel definition
        arguments={}: Parameter bindings: {name: number | expression | bool}
        preset='': Provenance only: the catalogue row whose values were written into `arguments`. The values are what the model uses, so editing one afterwards is fine and nothing re-reads the catalogue.
        """

    def sub_entry(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        port: str | dict[str, Any] = 'in',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a SUB_ENTRY block. Editor-only blocks (SUBMODEL, SUB_ENTRY, SUB_EXIT) that group blocks into a reusable sub-model with named ports, expanded away before the run. Editor-only placeholder shared by three types, never run directly -- `core/submodels.flatten()` expands them away before the engine builds any process. SUB_ENTRY: an input port of a submodel definition (`port` names it; exactly one outgoing link inside the definition, none incoming). SUB_EXIT: an output port of a definition (`port` names it; incoming links only). SUBMODEL: an instance of a definition dropped on the canvas (`definition` names it, `arguments` binds its parameters).

        port='in': Input port name of the definition
        """

    def sub_exit(
        self,
        name: str | None = None,
        *,
        description: str | None = None,
        position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_position: list[Any] | None = None,
        layout_rotation: float | None = None,
        label_offset: list[Any] | None = None,
        counter_offsets: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        port: str | dict[str, Any] = 'out',
    ) -> Block:
        """Add a SUB_EXIT block. Editor-only blocks (SUBMODEL, SUB_ENTRY, SUB_EXIT) that group blocks into a reusable sub-model with named ports, expanded away before the run. Editor-only placeholder shared by three types, never run directly -- `core/submodels.flatten()` expands them away before the engine builds any process. SUB_ENTRY: an input port of a submodel definition (`port` names it; exactly one outgoing link inside the definition, none incoming). SUB_EXIT: an output port of a definition (`port` names it; incoming links only). SUBMODEL: an instance of a definition dropped on the canvas (`definition` names it, `arguments` binds its parameters).

        port='out': Output port name of the definition
        """


class Model(Level):
    """A model written in Python; the file it produces is the model.

    Every block type is a method named after it, taking the block's parameters
    by name; ``a >> b`` links two blocks; ``add_resource`` and the like add an
    entry to a list of the file. Keywords are the run settings (``time_limit``,
    ``warmup``, ``replications``, every key of ``simulation``) and the top-level
    sections (``info``, ``network``, ``simulation``, ...).

    Parameters
    ----------
    **top
        Run settings and top-level sections. An unknown keyword raises
        ``TypeError`` naming the valid ones; a run setting given both as a
        keyword and inside ``simulation=`` raises too.

    See Also
    --------
    nagasim.build.export_python : The reverse, a model file as builder code.
    nagasim.modelfmt.minimal : The form ``to_dict`` returns.

    Examples
    --------
    >>> from nagasim import build as nb
    >>> m = nb.Model(time_limit=100, replications=2)
    >>> m.add_resource(name="Server")
    'Server'
    >>> arrive = m.create(distribution=nb.Exponential(rate=1.0))
    >>> serve = m.process(resource_name="Server", distribution=nb.Exponential(rate=1.25))
    >>> _ = arrive >> serve >> m.dispose()
    >>> [b["type"] for b in m.to_dict()["blocks"]]
    ['CREATE', 'PROCESS', 'DISPOSE']
    >>> m.run(seed=0).kpi("system/Total Entities Completed") > 0
    True
    """
    layout: dict[str, Any]
    def __init__(
        self,
        *,
        time_limit: float | None = ...,
        warmup: float = ...,
        replications: int = ...,
        deterministic: bool = ...,
        time_unit: Literal['milliseconds', 'seconds', 'minutes', 'hours', 'days', 'weeks', 'months', 'years'] = ...,
        length_unit: Literal['millimeters', 'centimeters', 'meters', 'kilometers'] = ...,
        currency: str = ...,
        energy_price: float | str = ...,
        entity_log_limit: int | None = ...,
        record_logs: bool = ...,
        timeseries_points: int | None = ...,
        timeseries_interval: float | None = ...,
        timeseries_counts: list[Any] = ...,
        sd: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        calendar: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        network: None | dict[str, Any] = ...,
        annotations: list[Any] = ...,
        background: None | dict[str, Any] = ...,
        building: None | dict[str, Any] = ...,
        info: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        initial_state: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        simulation: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        investment: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        dispatch: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        outputs: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        assertions: list[Any] = ...,
        path_checks: list[Any] = ...,
        dashboard: dict[str, Any] = ...,
        view: dict[str, Any] = ...,
    ) -> None:
        """A model written in Python; the file it produces is the model.

        time_limit: Run length in base time units; null/0 means "run until the model drains", which only works for a model that can drain.
        warmup: Statistics are reset at this time.
        replications: How many independent runs to make, each with its own seed.
        deterministic: Deterministic run mode: every distribution samples its mean, DECIDE-by-chance uses largest-remainder allocation, a failure's TTF/TTR use their mean, and replications are forced to 1. A mean-value model, not a fluid limit; agent-based and system-dynamics parts are unaffected.
        time_unit: The model's BASE unit; every duration without its own unit is already in it.
        length_unit: The model's length unit: default unit of new length fields and of reported lengths.
        currency: Cost label only; never enters the arithmetic.
        energy_price: Money per kWh, a number or an expression, evaluated once at run start. Prices every resource/fleet's "power".
        entity_log_limit: Cap on retained entity-log rows.
        record_logs: Record the per-EVENT entity/resource/store logs . False returns the three flat log frames empty and leaves every summary statistic exact -- what a big study wants.
        timeseries_points: How many samples the time-series OUTPUT sampler takes over a time-limited run (default 200).
        timeseries_interval: Fixed sampling interval for a draining run, which has no horizon to divide.
        timeseries_counts: Blocks whose departures the sampler counts, as the series block/<name>/out (flattened names). What a twin compares a plant counter with; none by default.
        sd: OPTIONAL system-dynamics step grid. Absent, rates change only at events. Present, every rate reading LEVEL(), FRACTION() or TNOW is re-integrated at each multiple of "dt"; static rates and events inside a step stay exact.
        calendar: OPTIONAL real dates. Absent, the clock is a plain number and nothing changes. Present, model time 0 is "start" and weekday schedules, dated series and dated orders resolve against it.
        """

    def add_scenario(
        self,
        *,
        name: str = ...,
        description: str = ...,
        goal: str = ...,
        overrides: list[Any] = ...,
    ) -> Any:
        """Add one entry to `scenarios` and return its name. Named parameter-override sets, run and compared by `nagasim.experiments`.

        name: The scenario's name, as the comparison table lists it.
        description: What the scenario changes and why; shown beside it in the editor.
        goal: Optional: the question this scenario answers, in a sentence. Shown in Present mode and the editor.
        overrides: The changes this scenario makes to the base model, applied in order.
        """

    def add_order(
        self,
        *,
        id: str = ...,
        description: str = ...,
        release: float | str = ...,
        due: float | str | None = ...,
